.TH DSP 4 "2026-06-30" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
dsp \- OSS (Open Sound System) audio device interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/soundcard.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B dsp
device is an OSS-compatible frontend onto the same audio backend that
drives the Sun/SADA
.BR audio (4)
nodes.  It lets programs written for the Open Sound System \(em native,
Linux, or FreeBSD \(em play PCM through Substrate's hardware drivers
without modification.
.PP
.B /dev/dsp
shares the backend with
.B /dev/audio
completely:
.BR open (2)
opens the backend,
.BR write (2)
queues PCM for playback, and
.BR close (2)
releases it.  Only the
.BR ioctl (2)
ABI differs \(em
.B dsp
speaks the OSS
.B SNDCTL_DSP_*
command set instead of the Sun/SADA
.B AUDIO_*
set.  The two frontends share the same exclusive-playback arbitration:
the first process to
.BR write (2)
either node claims the device, and a second process's
.BR write (2)
fails with
.BR EBUSY .
.SS Device Nodes
.TP
.B /dev/dspN
The OSS sample device for the
.IR N -th
audio device.
.TP
.B /dev/dsp
A convenience alias for
.BR /dev/dsp0 ,
registered when the first audio device appears.
.SS Default Configuration
On
.BR open (2)
the device is configured to the OSS convention of 8000 Hz,
.BR AFMT_U8 ,
mono.  Programs normally override this immediately with
.BR SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT ,
.BR SNDCTL_DSP_CHANNELS ,
and
.BR SNDCTL_DSP_SPEED .
.SS Sample Formats
The OSS
.B AFMT_*
formats map onto the framework encodings as follows:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_S16_LE
\(em signed 16-bit little-endian.
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_S16_BE
\(em signed 16-bit big-endian.
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_U8
\(em unsigned 8-bit.
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_S8
\(em signed 8-bit.
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_MU_LAW
\(em 8-bit \(*m-law.
.IP \(bu 4
.B AFMT_A_LAW
\(em 8-bit A-law.
.RE
.SS Cross-personality Dispatch
OSS programs from different operating-system personalities encode
.B SNDCTL_DSP_*
with their own
.B _IOWR
macros, so the direction and size bits in the high half of the request
word vary.  The
.B dsp
ioctl dispatch matches a command by its OSS group
.RB ( \(aqP\(aq )
and command number alone, ignoring those high bits, so every
personality's OSS ioctls reach the correct handler.
.SH IOCTLS
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_SPEED
Set the sample rate (arg: pointer to
.BR int ).
The actual rate the hardware accepted is written back.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT
Set the sample format to one of the
.B AFMT_*
values, or query the current format with
.BR AFMT_QUERY .
The format actually set is written back.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_CHANNELS
Set the channel count; the value used is written back.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_STEREO
Select mono (0) or stereo (1).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETFMTS
Return a bitmask of the supported
.B AFMT_*
formats.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETOSPACE
Fill a
.B struct audio_buf_info
describing the playback FIFO's free space (fragment size, total
fragments, free fragments, free bytes).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETISPACE
Fill a
.B struct audio_buf_info
for the capture buffer.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETBLKSIZE
Return the fragment size in bytes.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT
Hint the desired fragmentation (accepted and echoed back).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETCAPS
Return the device capability bits
.RB ( DSP_CAP_* ).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC
Drain queued playback (block until emitted).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_RESET
Discard queued playback immediately.
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_POST
Mark a playback boundary (no-op kick).
.TP
.B SNDCTL_DSP_GETODELAY
Return the number of bytes still queued for playback.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/dsp
Default OSS sample device (alias for
.BR /dev/dsp0 ).
.TP
.B /dev/dspN
OSS sample devices.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBUSY
Another process already holds the device for playback.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal was delivered while a
.BR write (2)
blocked waiting for the hardware to drain, before any data was queued.
.TP
.B EINVAL
An unsupported
.B SNDCTL_DSP_*
command number, or an invalid parameter.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR audio (4),
.BR uac (4)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B dsp
OSS frontend was added so OSS-targeted software (including FreeBSD SDL2
audio) could play through Substrate's audio drivers.  It is additive to
the Sun/SADA
.BR audio (4)
interface and shares its backend.
.SH BUGS
The interface is playback-oriented;
.BR read (2)
capture through
.B /dev/dsp
depends on the backing driver and is not implemented by all backends.
